WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.399
Hi I'm Matt Henderson and I like making

00:00:02.399 --> 00:00:04.400
mathematical animations;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.720
and recently I got a bit into making

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.880
animations that demonstrate chaos theory.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.000
So today I wanted to show a few chaos

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.630
theory animations.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.560
This one is Bouncing Balls and it's a

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.000
very simple system that

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.800
demonstrates chaos theory. 
So what we have is

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:27.599
actually two

00:00:27.599 --> 00:00:29.039
balls - you'll start to see it's two

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:31.359
balls once their paths diverge.

00:00:31.359 --> 00:00:33.200
After just a few bounces they're going

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:34.559
on totally different

00:00:34.559 --> 00:00:37.680
chaotic paths. At the beginning we've got-

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.399
they're on they're almost on the same

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:41.360
path-

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.719
(Brady: Almost-)
- They start off um right next to

00:00:44.719 --> 00:00:45.440
each other,

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.719
their initial positions are

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:51.440
equal up to the fourth decimal place. And

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.600
the point is like one of the hallmarks of

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.360
chaos is that there's this high

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:57.680
sensitivity to initial conditions where,

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.120
you know, even if it's four decimal

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.359
places off, the the

00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:03.359
difference between the two is

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:05.119
exponential over time until they are

00:01:05.119 --> 00:01:06.320
just

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.390
on totally different paths as it seems.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.680
This one is the key bounce really, yeah.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.600
When I posted this I kind of posed a

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.240
question, you know, isn't it surprising

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.520
that such a simple system is chaotic? And

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.680
of course the answer is no because

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.439
simple and chaotic aren't,

00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:28.479
like, opposites of each other. 
When I think of

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:30.479
chaos maybe I think of the weather, you

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:32.400
know, the butterfly effect.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.799
And that is a massively complex system

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:36.720
and that exhibits chaos;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.479
but it's not because it's complex that

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:40.159
it exhibits chaos, it's some more

00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:41.920
fundamental thing about the,

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.520
you know, the non-linearity of the

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.520
differential equations or 
something like that.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.770
This is in Mathematica again. it's

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:51.920
actually quite a simple 
piece of code that is basically

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:52.640
solving

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.960
the differential equation. We've got

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:55.840
four

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:57.840
equations, I could write them down as

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.920
well. The way it's written in the code

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.960
is like this, so y dash dash of t

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.560
equals minus g. So

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:09.840
the second derivative of the y position

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.640
is a function of t and that is minus g

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:13.440
which is the

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.920
gravitational constant. That's pretty

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.920
obvious right? Like the-

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.000
how much something is accelerating down

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.360
is just due to gravity.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.120
(So you're using the proper Earth's)

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.200
(gravity as your number there in the in)

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:25.920
(the animation?)

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.920
Yeah but I mean it doesn't really matter

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.200
because there's no

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.519
scale keys it's just some 
abstract circle in

00:02:31.519 --> 00:02:32.800
space but I could-

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.000
it happens to be like one metre wider

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.120
I think, or the radius would be one metre

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.360
but you know. I just have- I did put

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:42.640
in 9.81

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.360
as that number. And then we have a

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.640
similar one for x:

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.720
this is to say that there's no

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:49.680
acceleration

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.599
left and right. These ones are just the

00:02:51.599 --> 00:02:53.980
initial conditions here, 
these last four here.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.640
So we'll have- we basically have that x

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:57.360
and y

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.879
start off at x0

00:03:00.879 --> 00:03:04.239
and y0 which we will plug in. One ball

00:03:04.239 --> 00:03:04.959
will have

00:03:04.959 --> 00:03:07.760
a slightly different initial x0 than the

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:08.560
other ball does.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.959
And then the other one is just to say

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:12.319
that they start off

00:03:12.319 --> 00:03:14.239
stationary; so this is you know the

00:03:14.239 --> 00:03:16.000
derivatives

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.040
start off at (0,0). The speed

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.800
in the x direction is zero, speed in the

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.959
y direction is zero at the beginning.

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:25.440
So there's this there's this um sneaky

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:26.480
bit in here,

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.480
so this is saying when it happens that x

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.480
squared plus y squared equals 1,

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.360
which is the equation of the circle, then

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:34.080
I'm going to

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.599
change the derivative of x and y into

00:03:37.599 --> 00:03:40.280
the reflected values. Essentially now

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.720
Mathematica is going to take this

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.200
set of equations, it's going to look out

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.360
for that crossing point on the circle,

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.640
and reflect the speeds. So I'm plugging

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:51.440
in

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:54.920
x and y, so this is the first ball, x is

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.239
.001

00:03:56.239 --> 00:03:59.280
just off the centre and y is 0.5.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.560
And then for the second ball it's .0012,

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:04.159
so it differs in the fourth decimal

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:05.599
place. That's a

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:08.319
horizontal difference, so the x's are

00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:09.200
left and right.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.519
So if you run this code, now what it's

00:04:11.519 --> 00:04:13.120
done is it's computed

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.239
what these x and y positions are.

00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:17.919
So if I- if if I want to just sort of

00:04:17.919 --> 00:04:19.359
visualise those

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:21.280
that's what one of the balls' paths looks

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:22.800
like. And if I want to

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.040
plot the other one, you know, change this

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:26.840
to xf2,

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:32.160
yf2. So this is the whole solution.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.960
(So before you ever saw the animation you)

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:35.680
(saw this?)

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:39.840
Yeah that's right. Yeah.
- (That's a spoiler!)

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.560
Yeah! I mean before I saw this I saw a

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:43.840
whole bunch of like

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.320
red boxes saying 
'you got your code wrong';

00:04:47.199 --> 00:04:49.120
and maybe I saw a few where it didn't

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.720
detect the circle properly or the

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.910
reflection was wrong.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:03.360
This one is another simple yet chaotic

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.840
system. This one is highlighting the

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:06.880
difference between

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.039
something that isn't chaotic and then

00:05:09.039 --> 00:05:10.080
just a subtle change

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.320
that suddenly makes it chaotic. So here

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:13.199
we have two

00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:15.199
pool tables, one with perfectly straight

00:05:15.199 --> 00:05:17.199
edges and one that has

00:05:17.199 --> 00:05:21.280
walls that are slightly curved. Now

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.639
the balls all start moving off

00:05:24.639 --> 00:05:28.479
together in a group; 
and the balls aren't going to

00:05:28.479 --> 00:05:30.479
interfere with each other, they're just

00:05:30.479 --> 00:05:32.720
separate trajectories, modelled completely

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:34.240
independently. 
- (So they're kind of- the)

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.080
(balls are transparent to each other)
- Yeah

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:38.000
that's right. 
Obviously when they hit the wall

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.520
they're going to reflect.
The pictures look

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.600
pretty similar, actually these are nice,

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.919
slightly more spread out because of the

00:05:45.919 --> 00:05:48.479
sort of like a mirror, a bendy mirror or

00:05:48.479 --> 00:05:49.280
like a lens

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:50.880
kind of effect there.
- (Are we calling that)

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.800
(concave or convex?)

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.360
Yeah it should be convex. 
- (Convex to the table)

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:57.039
(but concave to the outside of the table?)

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:00.160
Yeah I guess everything that's convex is

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.800
also concave, depends on your perspective.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.039
And they bounce up here, and now you can

00:06:05.039 --> 00:06:06.080
really see that

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.000
things have- first of all there are balls

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.680
in a different part of the table.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.680
While these are still in a circular

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:13.199
shape that they started off in,

00:06:13.199 --> 00:06:15.759
these have nice spread out into more of

00:06:15.759 --> 00:06:21.840
an elliptical shape.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.909
Just as with the other bouncing balls

00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:28.400
soon they're just all spread out and

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.280
chaotic; and you know

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.280
if I if you were to see this picture

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:32.800
without the lines

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:35.759
and you were to say, well where did they

00:06:35.759 --> 00:06:36.800
start off?

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.680
You wouldn't even have a clue right? And

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:40.400
actually

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.759
in in a sense it's a fundamental

00:06:43.759 --> 00:06:45.280
fact of the mathematics that you would-

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:46.960
you couldn't have a clue because you

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:48.080
you wouldn't be able to measure with

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:50.639
enough precision if you let the time

00:06:50.639 --> 00:06:51.680
go on for long enough.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:52.800
(And the other ones will stay in that)

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.960
(circle forever?)
- Yeah this is a- you know,

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:56.400
this is obviously a mathematical

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.039
idealised pool table. What I wanted to

00:06:59.039 --> 00:07:00.080
get at was

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.440
what is the simplest 
change to a system that could

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.360
result in chaos? You know, what's

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:06.639
happening is that

00:07:06.639 --> 00:07:09.039
these bounces are more complex than

00:07:09.039 --> 00:07:10.400
these bounces which are

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.440
very linear. These ones are
non-linear and they

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.199
they start spreading things out. And

00:07:15.199 --> 00:07:16.880
there's a whole field of

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:19.360
studying these, they're called billiards

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:20.080
problems,

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:21.919
and you've got different types of

00:07:21.919 --> 00:07:23.199
billiards, different

00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:24.960
shapes and it's kind of interesting. In a

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:26.479
circle for example,

00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:28.960
things are not chaotic and they kind of

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:30.560
keep contracting in and out and bouncing

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:31.600
around the circle.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:38.720
The other- you know,
if you put like a square

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:39.759
in the middle of the circle it

00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:42.120
interferes with it and all of a sudden it is chaotic.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.919
(I wonder how long you would have to wait)

00:07:51.919 --> 00:07:54.000
(for that to return to a circle just by)

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:54.879
(pure chance.)

00:07:54.879 --> 00:07:57.840
There's a theorem that said that, if you

00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:59.599
wait long enough, that would happen.

00:07:59.599 --> 00:08:01.199
Yeah and obviously there's another point

00:08:01.199 --> 00:08:02.720
which is

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:04.479
what would happen in the real world?

00:08:04.479 --> 00:08:06.240
Like people when you're playing

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.240
pool, you're going to be in a world more

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:09.280
like this than

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.120
than this. You're not going to

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:12.800
have perfectly straight edges.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:15.199
So if you're looking at making a shot,

00:08:15.199 --> 00:08:16.400
you want to minimise the number of

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:17.520
bounces that have to go

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.160
exactly to your plan, because after you

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:20.879
know one,

00:08:20.879 --> 00:08:24.720
two, three, four, five bounces

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:27.120
things are way off trajectory from the

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:28.640
ideal world.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.519
This is a water wheel

00:08:37.519 --> 00:08:41.440
that is a model for the Lorenz Attractor

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:43.120
which you might have seen before as this

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:44.959
kind of like butterfly shaped

00:08:44.959 --> 00:08:48.240
3D thing that typifies a lot of chaotic

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:48.959
systems.

00:08:48.959 --> 00:08:52.740
What we've got is a 
water wheel with lots of

00:08:52.839 --> 00:08:55.920
these buckets that can 
collect water and we

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:57.600
have water flowing in from the top.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:00.320
It's not stable, so that when- if you

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:01.839
start filling up the top

00:09:01.839 --> 00:09:04.240
bucket then it will start spinning in

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:05.920
whatever direction it's

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:08.560
off by. So here we start filling it and it

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:10.800
starts to spin to the left.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.760
So another aspect of this wheel is that

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:16.720
the buckets are slowly leaking water,

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:19.839
which I haven't animated but

00:09:19.839 --> 00:09:21.680
you could imagine it as the water is

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:23.519
evaporating or just disappearing over

00:09:23.519 --> 00:09:26.080
time. And there's also a little bit of

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:27.940
friction to the wheel.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:33.600
These add up to giving a chaotic system

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:36.959
in that the movement of this dot here,

00:09:36.959 --> 00:09:39.920
which is the centre of mass of the wheel,

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:40.880
that movement

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.600
is chaotic. 
- (Centre of mass, that's the)

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:45.279
(centre of the mass of the wheel, okay.)

00:09:45.279 --> 00:09:48.480
Yeah that's right. And it- I mentioned

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:49.440
it's,

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:51.980
it's related to the Lorenz system.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:56.320
The Lorenz system has this classic

00:09:56.320 --> 00:09:58.080
kind of 3D butterfly

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:01.200
wing shape to it; and this is this is

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:03.680
kind of visible in the trace that the

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:06.800
centre of mass gives out.

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:08.880
Yeah it's kind of a coincidence, 
well it's a

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:10.800
total coincidence that the butterfly

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:11.519
effect

00:10:11.519 --> 00:10:14.320
is some aspect of chaos theory, and that

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:16.079
there's this butterfly wing

00:10:16.079 --> 00:10:19.200
shaped attractor that you get in a

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:21.519
bunch of chaotic systems like this,

00:10:21.519 --> 00:10:24.160
but you know it's kind of a nice title

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:26.320
as to say like visualising the butterfly

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:27.279
effect and

00:10:27.279 --> 00:10:28.640
I'm not sure if you really believe this

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:31.760
looks that much like a butterfly.

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:35.040
An Attractor is a space,
a subspace, inside a

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:36.480
3D space that things

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:39.040
end up converging onto. For- in this case

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:40.079
you could start the

00:10:40.079 --> 00:10:42.320
wheel with any kind of initial motion or

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:43.680
you could fill up the water

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:46.880
anywhere you wanted, but if you let it

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:49.760
evolve over time under its own laws of

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:51.040
physics, the point

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:53.519
will soon get onto this consistent kind

00:10:53.519 --> 00:10:55.610
of butterfly wing shape.

